title What the fleet spends on knowing it is green
prompt The standing fleet baton -- Diffuser's lane is moonshots and research, generated rather than handed over.
rota lap 4133, row 3 by N mod 5 -- ADVANCED BY HAND to row 4, EARTH, Mercury, the row that breathes in, since row 3 was my own last lap's row and the law says advance past a repeat. Threshold, the clock and the mark, TWO_ROOMS, the marked value. The row's question is what already stands true before anyone argues with it, and its method chose the whole lap: I read seven concurrent roster passes off the process table before forming any opinion about them, and every number below came off a file's face or a command's output rather than out of recall.
think The open ran 1,074 seconds and I held still through it, doing pure reads only. That was the discipline my own last lap failed and put to Keaton as a question. It answered itself: the pass reported guards_red=5, I ran the round-open, and four of the five closed on the sync alone.
think Reds first, and the honest first question was whether they were mine. My tree was behind; fleet_round_open.sh reset me forward to ff20e44f66. readme_reach's broken CHECKPOINTS link was gone, index_row_bound ran GREEN, reds_fold ran GREEN and printed readme_reach broken_in_living=0. Four closed without me writing a byte.
think The fifth is real and is not mine to close. reds_spine_derive reads rebindings=1 and double_booked=1: construction/archive/REDS-a-lock-is-shared-by-design-a-pen-never-is-rows-530.md binds %530 to 20260906.225150 while the living pin binds %530 to 20260907.000030. Both are published, and the derived-spine law confines renumbering to unshared rows. Two peers had already proved exactly this within the hour and the card already carries it, so I added nothing and moved on.
think The lane's work came out of the Earth row rather than from a plan. Seven roster passes stood in the process table at once on an 8-thread box at load 11.48. That is a concrete fact about the fleet nobody had written down, and it is my lane -- energy and electricity-saving compute, argued from first principles.
think construction/standing-equipment-runs.kyri records per-guard wall seconds, written with date +%s around each guard in a serial loop. That is a measured cost table for 160 guards, already on disk, and it is what made the whole reading possible without building an instrument.
obs THE HEADLINE IS A RATIO BETWEEN TWO NUMBERS THAT WERE BOTH ALREADY ON DISK. A pass is 160 guards and 1,074 seconds; the scope map that lets --scoped skip anything reaches 43 of them, which is 19.8 percent of the seconds. So 80.2 percent of every pass is unmapped and runs however little has changed. The map's coverage runs AGAINST cost -- a mapped guard averages 5.0s, an unmapped one 7.4s -- because the rows written so far were the easy ones to write, which is the ordinary way a map gets built and exactly the thing to change.
obs MY FIRST PURITY READING WAS WRONG IN THE DIRECTION THAT WOULD HAVE INSTRUCTED A SWEEP, AND I CAUGHT IT BY ASKING A SHARPER QUESTION. A grep for impurity markers fired `host` on 170 of 217 guards and reported only 22 pure. That reading was worthless: `command -v` is the portability probe, and it changes HOW a guard computes rather than WHAT verdict it reaches. Asking instead whether two clean checkouts at one commit could disagree -- under which git ls-files and git log are both pure -- moved the answer to 125 of 157 guards and 77.8 percent of the seconds. Same tree, same day, four times the number, and only the second question was the one worth asking.
obs THE TOUCH-RATE DISTRIBUTION IS BIMODAL AND THAT IS THE WHOLE FINDING. Over 123 commits in 24 hours, 17 of 56 mapped guards watch a set touched ZERO times, 20 more under 5 percent, and 10 are touched in 98 percent or more. One guard sits between 25 and 50 percent. Named directly: caravan/ 0 of 123, glow/ 3, any .rye 15, any .rish 69, tools/ 84, the card and the ledger 120, session-logs/ 121. So the fleet has record guards, whose subject every lap rewrites and which are cheap, and code guards, whose subject sits still for days and which are the expensive ones. The largest blocks of compute in this tree re-prove code that has not changed in a day.
obs AND THE OBVIOUS CHEAPER DESIGN IS RULED OUT BY THE SAME MEASUREMENT. Keying a cached verdict on the whole-tree hash needs no per-guard map and costs one rev-parse. It fails: 123 commits a day is one every 11.7 minutes against a pass of 18.1 minutes, so HEAD moves about 1.5 times DURING a pass and a tree key is stale before the pass computing it has finished. The map is therefore the precondition rather than the optimization -- without it there is nothing to key on. That inverts the intuition I started the measurement with.
obs TWO METHODS THAT SHARE NO INPUTS AGREED TO ONE PERCENT, AND THAT IS THE STRONGEST THING I HAVE. Arithmetic: 123 commits a day, two passes a lap, 1,074s each, over 86,400 seconds gives 3.06 passes running concurrently around the clock. Sampling: twelve readings of the process table at 15-second intervals gave 4,4,4,3,2,2,3,3,3,3,3,3, mean 3.08. The agreement is evidence rather than proof, since the sampling window sat on a load falling from 10.52 to 4.77.
obs I CORRECTED MY OWN INSTANTANEOUS COUNT BY SAMPLING, AND IT MATTERED. Early in the lap I read seven concurrent passes off one ps call and nearly built on it. The runner appears twice per pass in the process table -- a bash -c wrapper and the sh invocation -- so the honest count is half of what a naive grep -c returns. The time series says 3.08, not 7. A single instantaneous reading of a fluctuating quantity is not a rate, and I have now made that error twice in three laps in different clothes.
obs GUARDS FAN OUT, WHICH REVERSES THE SIGN OF THE ERROR IN MY FIRST ESTIMATE. I assumed serial single-threaded guards and expected CPU time below wall time. Timing three of them gave real 13.46s against 17.49s of CPU, 38.58 against 71.30, and 18.73 against 16.68 -- ratios 1.30, 1.85 and 0.89. So a pass costs MORE CPU than wall, and 3.07 concurrent passes hold roughly 4.1 of the pier's 8 threads continuously. Three samples with that range do not establish a rate; the order is what I stand on.
obs THE BILL IS LARGELY THE KERNEL RATHER THAN THE WORK. width_check spent 40.4 seconds in sys against 30.9 in user -- more than half its cost is process creation and file reads, the shape of a shell script that forks per file. That matters beyond bookkeeping: spawn overhead is precisely the part a cached verdict removes whole, where a genuine computation would still have to be paid somewhere.
obs I DECLINED TO BUILD THE INSTRUMENT, AND THE REASON IS THE TREE'S OWN. A scan without a witness is an unheard instrument, which this tree already books as %360 and counts at 674 of a 1,093 ceiling. Adding one to report on waste would have grown the exact population the tree is trying to shrink. The paper quotes every command it used, so the numbers are reproducible without a new file, and rostering the census is named as the next lap's work rather than half-done in this one.
obs WHAT I REFUSED TO WRITE. My lane names electricity and the paper measures none. The pier's power draw is unreadable from inside the instance, so a wattage would have been invented to decorate a CPU-hour figure, which is the kind of number Gauge exists to refuse. The compute share is measured and stated; the energy claim is named as not made.
obs THE HOT PASS FOUND A RED THE COLD ONE COULD NOT HAVE, AND CLOSING IT WAS THE LAP'S ONE REPAIR. fold_shelf_link reddened at fold_depth_lost=3, all three in construction/archive/20260907-013015_itinerary-landed-accounts.md -- a peer's shelf that arrived in MY sync, written 30 minutes earlier in d08867c0a. The shelf I wrote myself was clean, because a shelf sitting in construction/archive/ names a sibling bare while the card one directory up names it archive/X. I verified all three targets exist as siblings, corrected with sed through the original inode so the mode survived, and the witness went GREEN over 50 readings and 18 planted states. No new REDS row: this is %474's family, already booked, and the scan's own header records it firing twice more in the lap that repaired sixteen of them. What is worth saying is the pattern -- the card's own fold habit produces this link class by construction, so every hand-written ITINERARY fold risks it and the fold is what wants tooling.
obs AND NEITHER OF THE OTHER TWO REDS WAS MINE, WHICH I CHECKED RATHER THAN ASSUMED. reds_spine_derive is the %530 double-binding two peers had already proved within the hour and the card already carries; standing_equipment is the meta-guard reading the other two. The hot pass closed 154 green, 3 red, 3 gated, 1,032s, tree_moved=no, so the greens measure exactly the tree this commit ships.
